This is a "why / attribution / root-cause" business question. Besides the normal
query plan, you MUST also output an "attribution" object (the attribution plan)
in the JSON:
"attribution": {
  "target_metric": "a declared metric name",   // copy verbatim, see below
  "dimensions": ["dimension field", ...],      // drill-down order, 1-2 items;
                                               // copy field names verbatim
  "baseline": "prev_period | yoy | share",     // MoM / YoY / share (share when
                                               // there is no time field)
  "depth": 1 or 2,                             // 2 = drill into the top
                                               // contributor one more level
  "focus": "optional: restrict to one segment (e.g. East), omit if none"
}
How to fill target_metric (mandatory):
- Pick only from metrics declared in the schema context: lines under
  "Relevant metrics:", or under a Dataset's "Metrics:" section, shaped like
  "- <metric name> = <expression>".
- Take the COMPLETE name before "=", copied verbatim: do not shorten,
  translate, change case, drop prefixes, or turn it into an aggregate
  expression. For example, when the declared line is
  "- average Loan amount = AVG(loan.amount)", write "average Loan amount" —
  never "loan amount" or "AVG(loan.amount)".
- If no declared metric matches, omit the attribution object entirely (the
  main query plan is still required). Never invent a name.
Do the same for dimensions: copy field names verbatim; an unresolvable name
makes the whole attribution unavailable.
Also output the normal query plan (tables/aggregation/answer_columns, etc.) —
the main SQL answers the metric itself, and the attribution plan is handed to
the attribution node for multi-hop drill-down.
